  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will develop off the East Coast \nof Florida by Friday, and this low will pass by or through the \nregion Sunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will persist through Friday\nbefore shifting offshore on Saturday. Tranquil conditions will \ncontinue with above-normal temperatures around the region. \n\nMeanwhile, some broad troughing will continue to reside over \nthe Southeast US through late week. As some upper energy arrives\nwithin the sub-tropical jet-stream, a closed low will develop \nand strengthen to the east of Florida on Friday. This low is \nthen progged to gradually lift northward off the coasts of \nnorthern Florida, Georgia, and South Carolina on Saturday and \nnear the coast of North Carolina on Sunday.\n\nThere continues to be uncertainty among the 19/12Z suite of \nmodels. The operational GFS takes the low well inland, tracking \nalong the Appalachians and along the I-95 corridor Sunday \nafternoon and Sunday night before departing by Monday morning. \nThe AIGFS brings the low offshore east of Delaware late Sunday \nnight and the low tracks east of New Jersey by Monday morning \nbefore tracking out to sea on Monday. The operational ECMWF \ncontinues to spin off a secondary low out ahead of the primary \nlow on Saturday. This low will passes over the 40/70 benchmark \nSunday night, and then the primary low approaches Cape Hatteras \nlate Saturday night/early Sunday morning, and then tracks out to\nsea on Monday. The EC-AIFS has an inland track. The RRFS goes \nout to 00Z Monday and takes an inland track at the onset. \nWidespread rain tracks along the coast and moves into the region\nby Sunday morning, which is earlier than most guidance \nsuggests. The low is then just west of the Delmarva Peninsula by\nSunday evening.\n\nUnless the low ends up taking a far offshore track, conditions \nare setting up for widespread rain, heavy at times, late Sunday \nand Sunday night. There could even be a few embedded \nthunderstorms over southern Delaware and off the southeast New \nJersey coast Sunday evening.\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has a 30 percent chance of \nCyclone Formation in 48 hours (from 1 pm Thursday) and a 60 \npercent chance in the next 7 days. Regardless of whether this \nbecomes a tropical system and if it does, if it maintains \ntropical characteristics all the way up the coast, heavy rain, \ngusty winds mainly at the coasts, and even coastal flooding \nwould be possible. The Weather Prediction Center maintains a \nMarginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall for Delaware and southern \nNew Jersey.\n\nBehind the system, a strong cold front will then cross through \nthe region on Monday, bringing the return of high pressure to \nthe region mid-week with cooler temperatures.",
